INT. FLOWER MARKET COLD ROOM - PREDAWN

A walk-in refrigerator the size of a garage. Buckets of tulips and ranunculus line the metal shelves. The breath of four people hangs in the air. At the back, a steel door with a keypad.

NOOR BAKHSH (30s, delivery-driver uniform, clipboard) holds a flashlight. TIMOTEO "TIM" REYES (40s, in a florist's apron that doesn't fit) props a bucket against the wall. GWENDOLYN FOSS (60s, elegant, white gloves) studies the keypad. Behind them all, OSKAR LINDEN (20s, sweating despite the cold) carries a long cardboard flower box.

GWENDOLYN
Quiet, please. Everyone listen.

NOOR
We're listening.

GWENDOLYN
The vault door is on a six-digit code. We have three attempts before it locks for the hour.

TIM
Do we know the code?

GWENDOLYN
We know a code.

OSKAR
Is that the same as knowing it?

GWENDOLYN
Only if I am right.

Tim lowers his voice.

TIM
(to Noor)
How long until the shift change?

NOOR
Eleven minutes.

TIM
Eleven minutes. In a refrigerator. With roses.

NOOR
They're ranunculus.

TIM
(to himself)
Of course they are.

Gwendolyn removes one glove, finger by finger. Enters six digits. The keypad blinks red.

OSKAR
That's one.

GWENDOLYN
I can count, young man.

NOOR
What did you try?

GWENDOLYN
The florist's birthday.

TIM
That's me. I don't have a birthday.

GWENDOLYN
Nobody believes that.

She tries again. Red.

OSKAR
(whispering)
Two.

NOOR
Everyone stay calm. Gwendolyn, one more, and then we walk out as if nothing happened.

GWENDOLYN
We are not walking out.

She turns, takes the flower box from Oskar, and opens the lid. Inside, a small brass cylinder on a stand.

TIM
Is that what I think it is?

GWENDOLYN
It is a very polite drill.

OSKAR
That was always the plan?

GWENDOLYN
The code was a courtesy.

She sets the cylinder against the lock. A low whir fills the cold room. Frost trembles on the petals.

NOOR
Nine minutes.

GWENDOLYN
Plenty.
